ved, prepare the column that comes into it. Uses A, X and Y."} {:name :hide-unused-sprites, :doc "Move every sprite after the ones drawn this frame off the bottom of\n  the screen (a Y of 240 or more), which is the only way to hide one."} {:name :flush-column, :doc "Copy the column buffer into the PPU: in vertical blank (the NMI\n  handler does it), or with the picture off. Uses A and X."} {:name :unpack-to-video, :doc "Write packed bytes (see cartridj.screen/pack) to video memory,\n  from wherever PPUADDR points. :rt/pointer points at the packed bytes:\n  pairs of a count and a value, ending with a count of 0."} {:name :start-level, :doc "Put the player at the start, and every pickup and walker where the level has them."} {:name :ring-start, :doc "Where the ring of drawn columns should start, for where the camera is:\n  8 columns left of it, but not before the level's start or so far that\n  the ring would run off its end. Leaves it in A."} {:name :prepare-column, :doc "Work out the column of blocks :column/col into the column buffer: its\n  tiles (from the level's metatile numbers and the metatile tables), its\n  palette bytes, and where in the PPU they go. Uses A, X and Y."} {:name :move-player, :doc "Left and Right set the sideways speed, A jumps, and\n  platformer/move-body does the rest."} {:name :update-things, :doc "Every pickup and walker: move it if it moves, and see if the player is touching it."} {:name :draw-everything, :doc "The player: striding on every other eighth of a second while running\n  on the ground, otherwise standing. Then each pickup and walker."} {:name :solid-at, :doc "What kind of block is at a pixel? Give its x (16 bits: a position on\n  the level) in :rt/px, and its y in Y. Afterwards A is the kind (see\n  cartridj.screen/kinds), and the zero flag is set if it's empty.\n  Changes A and Y, and leaves X alone, so it can be asked about an object\n  while X is the object's slot."} {:name :walk, :doc "The walker whose slot is in X takes a step, a pixel every other frame,\n  and turns round at a wall (or the end of what it's standing on, if it\n  doesn't fall). One that falls drops 2 pixels a frame while there's\n  nothing under it: it started on a block's top, and 2 divides 16, so it\n  lands exactly on the next one down."} {:name :collect, :doc "The pickup whose slot is in X has been touched."}]}